# homelab-common (post-cutover #105): a size ceiling for EVERY log logrotate
# handles on this host.
#
# logrotate reads /etc/logrotate.d in name order, and a directive outside any
# stanza becomes the default for every stanza read after it. This file sorts
# first, so every later stanza (rsyslog's syslog, mail.log, auth.log, kern.log;
# apt, dpkg, haproxy, nginx, ...) gets this maxsize unless it sets its own size,
# maxsize or minsize. Verified with `logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.conf`: the
# rsyslog stanza reports "log files >= 209715200 are rotated earlier".
#
# Why: the stock rsyslog stanza is weekly, four generations, delaycompress. At the
# 1.3 GB a week a health-check flood wrote to mail.log and syslog, two uncompressed
# weeks of both filled a 7.8 GB root disk (prod-homelab39, 2026-11-26). With this
# a log that passes 200 MB rotates at the next daily logrotate run whatever its
# schedule says; a quiet log still rotates on its schedule, with the same number
# of generations. It bounds the damage of the next flood; the flood itself is
# fixed at its source (homelab-postfix 0.1.33, homelab-dovecot 0.1.22,
# homelab-haproxy 0.1.15, the DNS Lua records' interval).
maxsize 200M
